    {
      "turn": 2,
      "doctor_question": "Did you have similar trouble staying organized or paying attention when you were a child?",
      "targeted_symptom": "additional_requirement_1",
      "patient_response": "Oh, gosh, yes. Even way back in grade school. I remember my teachers were always telling my mother that I was a bright girl, but just completely scattered. I was always talking out of turn, blurting things out in the middle of a lesson. Like in Mrs. Gable's third-grade class—she was this very tall woman who always wore these heavy, clunky turquoise necklaces. I love turquoise, actually. My husband bought me a beautiful turquoise ring when we went to New Mexico for our tenth anniversary. We took a road trip, and it was so incredibly hot there, I honestly don't know how people stand the heat in the desert. We went to this little roadside diner and had the absolute best cherry pie, though, the crust was so flaky... \n\nWait, what were we talking about? Oh, right, being a kid. Yeah, so I was always losing my homework papers, and the teachers constantly had to give me reminders to finish my work because I'd just drift off or start doing something else. It drove my mother crazy.",
