    {
      "turn": 3,
      "doctor_question": "Before age 12, did you already have these attention and organization problems?",
      "targeted_symptom": "additional_requirement_1",
      "patient_response": "Oh, definitely before I was 12. Like I was just saying about grade school, it was always the exact same story. The teachers would tell my mother I was bright, but just completely scattered. Always losing my papers, and they were constantly having to give me reminders just to finish my work. And the talking out of turn! I was always blurting things out. \n\nI still do that, honestly. Just last week at the grocery store—the big one on 4th street, they just remodeled the produce section, it's so open and gorgeous now—I saw a woman buying those out-of-season strawberries in the plastic clamshells. You know the ones that look huge but taste like absolutely nothing? I just blurted right out, \"Oh honey, don't waste your money, they taste like water!\" She looked at me like I was crazy. I felt terrible, but it just popped out of my mouth before I could stop it. I really need to mind my own business. The produce manager there is a sweetheart, though, he always sets aside the good peaches for me in July. Peaches make the absolute best cobbler, especially if you add just a tiny pinch of nutmeg to the crust... \n\nWait, did I answer you? You asked about how old I was, right? Yes, definitely way before I turned 12, they were always having to remind me to stay on track.",
